                "title": "consumer_credit_policy.pdf",
                "url": null,
                "publishedAt": null,
                "snippet": "Consumer Credit Policy\n3.2 Credit score bands, debt-to-income and loan-to-value limits\nEvery applicant is placed in a credit band using the middle of the three bureau scores, or the single score when only one is available. The band sets the maximum debt-to-income ratio (DTI) and the maximum loan-to-value ratio (LTV) the Bank will accept. Table 3 shows the limits that apply to applications taken on or after 1 July 2026.\nExceptional, Credit score = 780 and above. Exceptional, Maximum DTI = 45%. Exceptional, Maximum auto LTV = 120%. Exceptional, Maximum mortgage LTV = 97%. Exceptional, Pricing tier = A. Very Good, Credit score = 720 to 779. Very Good, Maximum DTI = 43%. Very Good, Maximum auto LTV = 115%. Very Good, Maximum mortgage LTV = 95%. Very Good, Pricing tier = B. Good, Credit score = 670 to 719. Good, Maximum DTI = 40%. Good, Maximum auto LTV = 110%. Good, Maximum mortgage LTV = 90%. Good, Pricing tier = C. Fair, Credit score = 620 to 669. Fair, Maximum DTI = 36%. Fair, Maximum auto LTV = 100%. Fair, Maximum mortgage LTV = 85%. Fair, Pricing tier = D. Poor, Credit score = 619 and below. Poor, Maximum DTI = 30%. Poor, Maximum auto LTV = 90%. Poor, Maximum mortgage LTV = 80%. Poor, Pricing tier = E\nTable 3. Underwriting limits by credit band (version 5.1, effective 1 July 2026)",
                "metadata": {
